WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.350
Vocabulary, active vocabulary,
passive vocabulary.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.480
When do we know a word?

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:07.740
This comes up all the time.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.210
You know, I've often said that the
most important task in language

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.040
journey is acquiring words.

00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:18.855
Of course phrases are always, are also
important, but phrases consist of words,

00:00:19.665 --> 00:00:26.205
and I use the word acquire in the sense
of developing a passive vocabulary.

00:00:26.595 --> 00:00:31.095
So I, for example, saw a comment on, on
my YouTube channel here from one of my

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.025
listeners who said, The most important
thing is your active vocabulary.

00:00:35.565 --> 00:00:38.805
And, and often people say,
you know, if you don't use it,

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.185
you have to use it right away.

00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:43.575
If you don't use it right away, you,
you won't learn it, you'll lose it.

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:48.735
And I have always resisted that for
a simple reason, and that is that

00:00:48.945 --> 00:00:54.120
if you are taught something today,
and you're tested on it today or

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.790
tomorrow, you may in fact remember it.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:02.250
That doesn't mean that you have acquired
that vocabulary item, doesn't mean that

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.010
you will remember it a few months later.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:08.910
I've, uh, stressed always that
the process of acquisition is

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.100
much le much fuzzier than that.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.410
We're kind of putting
words into our brain.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.090
Some of which activate
sooner, some activate later.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.020
I don't make much of a distinction
between active and passive vocabulary.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:29.370
If anything, I believe in accumulating
as much passive vocabulary as possible

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.680
because that is the first step and the key
step to eventually being able to use them.

00:01:35.039 --> 00:01:39.150
And if you don't have this passive
vocabulary, if you don't have

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.660
comprehension, it's very difficult
to have conversations with

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.120
people because most people, if...

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.630
especially you're speaking to native
speakers, they will have a much larger

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:53.100
vocabulary than you have and even in
your own language, you have a much larger

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.180
passive vocabulary than active vocabulary.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.340
So the passive vocabulary is, is
an important part of, you know,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.080
where you are in the language.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.560
And we shouldn't just focus on
what we are able to use today,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.150
may not be able to use tomorrow.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:12.380
Uh, it's far more important just
to continue filling your brain with

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.260
words that will eventually activate.

00:02:15.405 --> 00:02:19.965
Also when it comes to like, when can
I claim that I have this passive,

00:02:19.965 --> 00:02:21.524
even the passive vocabulary?

00:02:22.035 --> 00:02:25.665
Uh, I was talking to someone the other day
who said, maybe, you know, at LingQ you

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.165
should, uh, you know, have some kind of
an indicator of when we have learned, for

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.905
example, uh, the names of family members,
which in some languages is quite complex.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.769
Or the names of the colors.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:42.660
Well, even leaving aside the fact that
those things are very difficult to learn

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.880
and not all that useful out of context,
you'll only acquire them gradually.

00:02:48.359 --> 00:02:50.130
But even if you...

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.119
how do you know that?

00:02:51.119 --> 00:02:55.200
Just because I saw, you know, if
I'm on LingQ and I saw this, uh,

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:01.700
word which represented, you know, my
mother-in-law and my, you know, somebody

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.290
else's side of the family or whatever.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.070
Some of these languages have very
complicated words for cousins and

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.320
brothers and older and nephews.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.270
But even if I remember on one
occasion, it doesn't mean that I'll

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.100
remember them on another occasion.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:21.830
Far more important is to keep track of,
and this is what we do at LingQ, the

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:26.450
fact that people are continuing to expose
themselves to the language, are continuing

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:32.700
to, you know, pile words and phrases
into their brain increasingly creating

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:40.020
these sort of neural connections, which
at some point will a, create therefore

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:45.000
an active bit of vocabulary that you
just naturally recognize you, not because

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.290
you're tested on it, but because you
naturally start to recognize it in your.

00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:54.325
Reading and listening, uh, often that'll
mean at LingQ that it's a white word now

00:03:54.325 --> 00:03:56.095
it's no longer highlighted in yellow.

00:03:56.245 --> 00:03:57.805
You just naturally know what it means.

00:03:57.805 --> 00:03:58.975
You don't even think about it.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.480
Or at least you know it insofar
as a certain context is concerned.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.140
You may not be aware of the full range
of meaning of that word, but you at

00:04:07.144 --> 00:04:09.209
least are aware of it in that context.

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:12.540
Eventually, as you see that word in
different contexts, you'll become

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:18.180
aware of the sort of broader scope of
meaning of that word, but testing you

00:04:18.185 --> 00:04:20.700
on, you know, what does this mean?

00:04:21.075 --> 00:04:26.685
Now to me is not a, um, a meaningful,
uh, sort of measure of where you

00:04:26.685 --> 00:04:30.794
are, but it is a sort of reflection
of, of how languages are taught at

00:04:30.794 --> 00:04:34.635
school or constantly being tested so
that the teacher can show the parents

00:04:34.635 --> 00:04:37.484
and the school administrators that
the kids are learning something.

00:04:37.815 --> 00:04:41.505
And after 10 years of that kind of
language instruction, very often the

00:04:41.505 --> 00:04:42.945
kids still can't speak the language.

00:04:43.215 --> 00:04:46.860
Far better to just load it
all in there, load it all in

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.600
there, let it sort itself out.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:54.450
When the opportunity arises, when
the need arises, lo and behold things

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.200
start to activate both the active
vocabulary, which was hiding there

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.390
somewhere, and the passive vocabulary.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.790
So just a bit of a, sort of a, it's,
it's very much a theme that I've, uh,

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.530
you know, referenced in the past and
that is, let's not be too concerned

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.600
about like, do you really know the word?

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.940
Do you know the full scope
of meaning of the word?

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.710
Are you able to use the word?

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:17.820
Don't worry about that.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.100
Just keep on shoveling it into your brain,
and eventually you will find that you'll

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.810
be able to understand these words and some
of them, but always a smaller subset you

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:30.630
will be able to use when the need arises.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:31.890
Thanks for listening.

